Hardware Wiring Guide
Components Needed
Arduino Uno board

PIR Motion Sensor (e.g., HC-SR501)

Piezo Buzzer (or speaker)

LED (with a 220Ω–330Ω resistor)

Breadboard and Jumper Wires

Step-by-Step Connections
Power Rails: Connect the 5V and GND pins on your Arduino to the power rails on your breadboard.

PIR Motion Sensor:

Connect the VCC pin to the 5V rail.

Connect the GND pin to the GND rail.

Connect the Signal / Output pin to Arduino Pin 2.

LED Indicator:

Connect the anode (longer/positive leg) of the LED through a current-limiting resistor to Arduino Pin 3.

Connect the cathode (shorter/negative leg) to the GND rail.

Buzzer:

Connect the positive (red) wire of the buzzer to Arduino Pin 4.

Connect the negative (black) wire to the GND rail.

Tips and Tricks
PIR Sensor Calibration: Most PIR sensors have two small potentiometers (dials) on the back to adjust sensitivity and time delay (how long the sensor stays HIGH after detecting motion). Adjust these to fine-tune your alarm's response time!

False Triggers: PIR sensors need a brief warmup period (usually 30–60 seconds) when first powered on to stabilize. Keep this in mind during testing if the alarm triggers immediately upon startup.
